WHAT: Adds a final optimization cleanup pass after the last inline round.

WHY: After inlining, new optimization opportunities may emerge (dead code,
constant folding) that the earlier passes didn't see. This adds one more
cleanup sweep to catch those.

GAS IMPACT: None on rosetta contracts.

TEAM NOTES: Correct in theory but the inlined code doesn't produce patterns
that the cleanup pass recognizes. The remaining overhead in these contracts
comes from deeper issues (ABI decode patterns, stack scheduling) that
simple cleanup passes can't address. This and step 6 together represent
the end of diminishing returns from pipeline expansion.
